          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The metals in Daniel 2 represent a succession of earthly or Gentile kingdoms, ultimately destroyed and superseded by God's everlasting kingdom symbolized by the stone.\",\"book_summary\":\"The image represents successive Gentile world-kingdoms shattered by the stone-kingdom of God.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Metals/image = successive human world kingdoms; stone = God's eternal kingdom that overcomes them.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Metals/image = successive Gentile world-kingdoms; stone = God's kingdom that shatters them.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"None material. The book does not identify the individual empires, whereas the consensus discusses competing historical identifications, but this omission does not change the shared symbolic referents.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None.\",\"rationale\":\"Both readings reduce to precisely the interpretation explicitly supplied in Daniel 2: a sequence of worldly kingdoms represented by the image's materials, followed by their destruction and replacement by God's enduring stone-kingdom. Calling the kingdoms 'Gentile' is conventional contextual precision rather than a substantive narrowing or reclassification.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"One might argue that 'Gentile' narrows the broader consensus category of earthly kingdoms, but in the Daniel 2 context the successive kingdoms under discussion are conventionally understood as Gentile imperial powers, so no meaningful case is excluded.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
